$TRADOOR  One general's success costs countless bones! How to help men trading cryptocurrencies get back to normal life?
I am not a big shot in the crypto world; this bit of profit is just a drop in the bucket for real big shots, just an old leek who has blown up positions and stepped into countless pits.
#zec  Last year, a brother of mine came to me with 2700 USD and said he wanted to earn back the losses he had before. I didn't talk to him about moving averages, MACD, or those complex things; I just gave him three pieces of experience that I had learned the hard way.
#ETH  He followed my advice for three months, and his account surged to 50,000 USD without blowing up once. How much you can grasp these three "life-saving rules" all depends on #beat  your respect for the market. ​
Money is divided into three parts: 2700 USD split into three transactions of 900 USD each, not to be used interchangeably.
The first part is for short-term trading; max two positions opened daily, finish the software management;
The second part waits for trends; if there is no bullish pattern on the weekly chart and no volume breaking key levels, keep it in cash;
The third part is for emergency funds; when the market spikes and there's a risk of blowing up positions, add more to avoid losing all principal.
Only trade with the trend: recognize three entry signals — if the daily moving average is not bullish, keep cash;
If the market breaks previous highs with volume and the daily closes steadily, then enter with a small position; if profits reach 30% of the principal, withdraw half of the profits and set a 10% trailing stop for the rest to secure profits.
Lock emotions: write a trading plan before entering the market, set a stop loss at 3%, and let it automatically close at that point;
If profits reach 10%, pull the stop loss to the breakeven point; shut down the computer at midnight every day; if you can't sleep, uninstall the app to avoid emotional trading.
The market has opportunities every day; without capital, there are no chances. First, get these three rules right, then ponder waves and indicators. Those who have been through the rain know best how to hold an umbrella for others; it's better to enjoy together than alone, you reach out your hand, and I will pull you to shore @Square-Creator-58b326784

